#!/usr/bin/env zsh
# ONE global queue for heavy Rust builds / test suites / benchmarks.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   Several agents working in parallel each want to run a full test suite.
#   Those runs do not overlap usefully on one machine -- they thrash. CPU
#   oversubscription makes every suite slower than if it had run alone, and
#   memory is the real cliff: a heavy job at full parallelism can peak high
#   enough RSS that a second concurrent one means swap, and swap means
#   suites that never finish. QUEUE_SLOTS (see below) is a per-machine value
#   exported from .zshenv, default 1; raise it only after measuring peak
#   suite RSS on THAT machine, never by assumption from another box's numbers.
#
#   MEASURED 2026-07-20 on parot-core (2,269 tests), on the box that measurement
#   was taken on: one `cargo nextest run` consumed 2,930s of CPU against 323s of
#   wall clock -- ~9x parallelism. A single suite ALREADY saturates a box at
#   nextest's default per-job thread count (one test process per logical CPU);
#   nextest's per-job threads are additionally capped by NEXTEST_TEST_THREADS
#   (cpus / QUEUE_SLOTS, set in .zshenv) so concurrent queued suites can't
#   oversubscribe whatever machine is running them.
#
#   This is a thin wrapper over task-spooler (`ts`), which is a real job queue
#   daemon -- FIFO ordering, slot-limited admission, job listing, output
#   capture. The wrapper pins ONE global socket (so every agent in every repo
#   shares the same queue), sets the slot count, keeps the job record `ts` does
#   not, and makes the whole thing a transparent command prefix.
#
# QUEUEING IS EXPLICIT -- YOU TYPE THE PREFIX
#   Until 2026-08-03 a PreToolUse hook matched heavy commands and rewrote them
#   into this queue automatically. It no longer does, and the machinery that
#   existed to make auto-queueing tolerable (a weight classifier, a unit budget)
#   went with it. What remains is a hook that DENIES an unqueued suite rather
#   than silently rewriting it, so forgetting the prefix is an error you can see
#   instead of a collision you cannot. See SLOTS below.
#
# RELATIONSHIP TO cargo-slot -- THEY SOLVE OPPOSITE PROBLEMS, DON'T COMBINE
#   cargo-slot gives each agent its OWN target dir so agents can compile
#   CONCURRENTLY (cargo takes an exclusive lock on target/).
#   queue decides WHICH jobs may overlap at all.
#   Under queue, agents should SHARE one warm target/ per worktree. Use
#   cargo-slot only when you want two builds of the SAME worktree at once.
#
# USAGE
#   queue <command...>             wait for a free slot, then run <command> here
#   queue '<shell string>'         one quoted arg is run via `zsh -c`
#   queue --solo <cmd...>          same, but take EVERY slot: run alone
#   queue --priority <cmd...>      same, but jump the queue
#   queue --events <cmd...>        emit machine-readable state lines
#   queue -l | --list              show the queue (waiting / running / finished)
#   queue --watch                  live-refreshing queue view
#   queue --status [id|--last]     show the full job record (exit code, timings)
#   queue --exit-code [id|--last]  print just that job's exit code
#   queue --ahead                  how many jobs a new submission waits for
#   queue --slots [N]              show, or set, how many jobs may run at once
#   queue --cancel <id|label>      drop a QUEUED job (never kills a running one)
#   queue --triage                 report duplicate / overlapping / needless jobs
#   queue --clear                  forget finished jobs
#   queue --kill                   stop the queue daemon (not a running job)
#
# EXAMPLES
#   queue cargo nextest run --workspace
#   queue 'cd ../other-worktree && cargo nextest run'   # see THE `&&` TRAP
#   queue --solo cargo bench --bench x
#   queue -- cargo build --release        # `--` if the command starts with a flag
#   queue --exit-code --last              # ground truth after piping output
#   queue --triage && queue --cancel 41   # what is redundant, then drop it
#
# SEMANTICS
#   Blocks until there is a free slot, then runs the command IN THIS PROCESS:
#   stdout and stderr stream live and stay separate, the exit code is the
#   command's own, and the job inherits this shell's cwd, stdin and
#   environment. It is a queue, not a sandbox -- `queue X` behaves exactly like
#   `X`, only it waits its turn.
#
#   Because it BLOCKS, a caller with a timeout (an agent's shell tool caps at
#   10 minutes) should run it detached / in the background.
#
# THE `&&` TRAP -- THE ONE THING A PREFIX CANNOT DEFEND AGAINST
#       queue cd /repo && cargo nextest run     # WRONG
#   The shell splits on `&&` before this script is ever exec'd, so `queue`
#   receives only `cd /repo`, queues THAT, exits -- and the suite then runs
#   completely unqueued. Nothing inside this file can detect it; the operator
#   never arrives. Two defences exist, and you want both:
#     1. Pass the whole thing as ONE quoted argument. A single argument
#        containing shell syntax is run via `zsh -c`, so quoting is all it takes:
#            queue 'cd /repo && cargo nextest run'
#     2. `unqueued_heavy_guard.py` (PreToolUse) sees the raw command string an
#        agent submitted, which is the only vantage point from which the mistake
#        is visible at all, and denies it with the corrected form.
#   The same applies to `|`, `;` and `>`; a leading `FOO=bar` is fine either way,
#   since the assignment is exported into this process and inherited by the job.
#
# EXIT CODES ARE RECORDED, NOT JUST RETURNED
#   `queue cmd | tail` returns TAIL's status, so a suite with failing tests
#   reads as "exit code 0". That is a shell fact no wrapper can change -- so
#   every job also writes a record, and the code can be read back afterwards:
#
#       queue cargo nextest run --workspace 2>&1 | tail -20
#       queue --exit-code --last            # <- ground truth
#
#   `queue --status --last` shows the whole record: command, cwd, slot cost,
#   queued/started/finished times and the exit code.
#
# SLOTS -- THE WHOLE SCHEDULING MODEL
#   QUEUE_SLOTS (default 1) jobs run at once. Every job costs one slot;
#   `--solo` costs all of them, so it runs with the machine to itself. That is
#   the entire policy. There is no weight table and nothing inspects your
#   command to decide what it is.
#
#   WHY THIS REPLACED A WEIGHTED BUDGET, since the reverse migration is recorded
#   in this file's history and looks like a regression:
#     The old model auto-queued via a PreToolUse hook that rewrote every
#     matching command. Because `cargo check` was FORCED into the queue, it
#     could sit 9 minutes behind a suite under a flat FIFO -- so a weighted
#     budget was invented to let a check (3) overlap a suite (9) inside a
#     12-unit budget, and a classifier was needed to tell them apart.
#     Queueing is now explicit. Nobody types `queue cargo check`, so it never
#     enters the queue, so it cannot queue behind anything -- the latency the
#     weights bought is recovered by NOT queueing rather than by weighing. The
#     classifier and the budget arithmetic both became answers to a question
#     that is no longer asked.
#
#   WHY THE DEFAULT IS 1 AND NOT 2
#     A single suite already runs at ~9x parallelism on 10 cores on the box
#     that was MEASURED 2026-07-20 (2,930s CPU / 323s wall). Memory is the
#     real cliff on any machine -- a heavy job at full parallelism can peak
#     close to that machine's usable RAM, so two heavy jobs at once mean swap,
#     and swap means suites that never finish. QUEUE_SLOTS is a per-machine
#     value exported from .zshenv (default 1) -- raise it only after measuring
#     peak suite RSS on THAT machine, and size NEXTEST_TEST_THREADS (also set
#     in .zshenv, as cpus / QUEUE_SLOTS) accordingly so a raised slot count
#     doesn't also raise per-job thread oversubscription. Since nothing
#     classifies commands any more, a slot count of 2 means "two SUITES may
#     overlap", which is precisely the collision this tool exists to prevent.
#     Prefer `--solo` to lowering it again.
#
# WHY NOT FAST-PATH AN EMPTY QUEUE
#   "Nothing is running, so just exec directly" is a check-then-act race: two
#   agents both observe an empty queue and both start heavy jobs, unaccounted,
#   which is exactly the collision this exists to prevent. The saving would be
#   a few milliseconds of socket round-trip against jobs measured in minutes.
#   Deliberately not implemented.
#
# HEARTBEAT
#   While waiting, a "still queued" line goes to stderr every QUEUE_HEARTBEAT
#   seconds (default 30; 0 disables). It names the job blocking you and
#   estimates the wait from that command's median runtime. Without it the
#   wrapper goes silent for however long the queue is -- which reads as a hang
#   and provokes callers into re-running the job. QUEUE_QUIET=1 silences it.
#
# --events MODE
#   For callers that want NOTIFICATIONS rather than live output (an agent's
#   Monitor tool turns each stdout line into an event):
#
#     QUEUED id=7 ahead=2 label=tq-1234-… slots=1 cost=1
#     RUNNING id=7 waited=312s
#     DONE id=7 exit=0 elapsed=522s out=/tmp/ts-out.XXXX
#
#   TRADEOFF, on purpose: events mode submits the job DETACHED, so the
#   command's own output is captured to `out=` instead of streaming live. You
#   get notifications or you get streaming, not both.
#
# RE-ENTRANCY
#   A queued job runs with QUEUE_ACTIVE=1. If queue sees that set it runs the
#   command directly instead of enqueuing it. Without this, a queued `just
#   test` whose recipe itself calls queue would wait forever for the capacity
#   its own parent is holding -- a guaranteed self-deadlock.

emulate -L zsh
# no_multios matters: with zsh's default MULTIOS, `2>&1 1>&3` does not move a
# stream, it TEES it, so the capture path below would silently copy stdout into
# the stderr file and a caller's `2>/dev/null` would start eating real output.
setopt no_unset pipe_fail no_multios

readonly SELF="${0:t}"

die() { print -u2 "$SELF: $*"; exit 1 }

# --- already inside a queued job: run directly, never re-enqueue -------------
# Management subcommands still work in here; only an actual command bypasses
# the queue. `--` must be handled explicitly, else `queue -- cargo x` would
# look like a flag, fall through, and deadlock on its parent's capacity.
if [[ -n "${QUEUE_ACTIVE:-}" ]]; then
  case "${1:-}" in
    -h|--help|-l|--list|status|--watch|--slots|--budget|--clear|--kill) ;;
    --cancel|--triage) ;;
    --status|--exit-code|--ahead|--ahead-of|"") ;;
    # A LOOP, not a single shift: submission flags stack (`--solo --priority`),
    # and stripping only the first would exec `--priority cargo test` -- i.e.
    # try to run a binary literally named `--priority`. This branch shifted
    # once, which was correct only while there were two flags and nobody
    # combined them. Exact mirror of the submission-side parser further down,
    # including stopping AT `--`: everything after it is the command, even if
    # it looks like one of our own flags.
    --events|--priority|--solo|--)
      while [[ "${1:-}" == (--events|--priority|--solo) ]]; do shift; done
      [[ "${1:-}" == "--" ]] && shift
      [[ $# -gt 0 ]] && exec "$@" ;;
    *)  exec "$@" ;;
  esac
fi

# ── state layer ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `ts` tracks a job list; it does not track cwd, target dir, submitter, weight
# submitter or exit history. Everything built on top -- the exit-code ground truth,
# the ETA, abandon reaping, anti-affinity -- needs those, so queue keeps its own
# record beside the queue.
#
# Records are APPEND-ONLY `key=value` lines and readers take the LAST value for
# a key. Two writers (this process and its background supervisor) can therefore
# both update one record with no locking and no lost updates.
readonly STATE="${QUEUE_STATE:-${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/queue}"
readonly JOBS="$STATE/jobs"
readonly BY_ID="$STATE/by-id"
readonly LASTD="$STATE/last"
readonly HISTORY="$STATE/history.tsv"

state_init() { mkdir -p "$JOBS" "$BY_ID" "$LASTD" 2>/dev/null || true }

# One record (plus captured output) per job, forever, would quietly turn into
# thousands of files and an unbounded history. Prune on submission, at most
# once a day, so the cost never lands on an interactive `--status`.
prune_state() {
  local stamp="$STATE/.pruned"
  [[ -f "$stamp" && -z "$(find "$stamp" -mtime +1 2>/dev/null)" ]] && return 0
  : >"$stamp"
  find "$JOBS" "$BY_ID" -type f -mtime +7 -delete 2>/dev/null
  if [[ -r "$HISTORY" ]] && (( $(wc -l <"$HISTORY") > 5000 )); then
    tail -n 2000 "$HISTORY" >"$HISTORY.new" 2>/dev/null && mv "$HISTORY.new" "$HISTORY"
  fi
  return 0
}

# Exact whole-line membership. Substring matching would be wrong in both places
# this is used: /repo/target substring-matches /repo/target2, and one session id
# can be a prefix of another -- either would silently mis-schedule.
contains_line() { print -r -- "$1" | grep -qxF -- "$2" }

# Who submitted this. `--last` means "the job *I* just ran", so this key has to
# survive the very shape the feature exists for: `queue cmd | tail` puts queue
# in a subshell, so $PPID is that subshell -- gone by the time you ask for the
# exit code. In order of reliability:
#   1. QUEUE_SESSION -- injected by the PreToolUse hook, stable across an
#      agent's tool calls, and the only key that distinguishes concurrent agents.
#   2. the controlling tty -- stable for a human, across pipelines and subshells.
#   3. the process group -- stable across pipelines in non-interactive shells.
#   4. $PPID -- last resort.
compute_session() {
  [[ -n "${QUEUE_SESSION:-}" ]] && { print -r -- "$QUEUE_SESSION"; return }
  local t g
  t=$(ps -o tty= -p $$ 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')
  [[ -n "$t" && "$t" != "??" && "$t" != "?" ]] && { print -r -- "tty-${t//\//-}"; return }
  g=$(ps -o pgid= -p $$ 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')
  [[ -n "$g" && "$g" != 0 ]] && { print -r -- "pgid-$g"; return }
  print -r -- "shell-$PPID"
}
readonly SESSION="$(compute_session)"
session_id() { print -r -- "$SESSION" }

rec_put() { print -r -- "$2=$3" >>"$1" 2>/dev/null || true }

# Last value wins, so a later append supersedes an earlier one. A value may
# itself contain '=', hence the substr rather than a field split.
rec_get() {
  [[ -r "$1" ]] || return 1
  local v
  v=$(awk -v k="$2" 'index($0, k "=")==1 {v=substr($0, length(k)+2); f=1} END{if(f) print v}' "$1")
  [[ -n "$v" ]] || return 1
  print -r -- "$v"
}

# A job id -> its record path. Also accepts a label, and `--last` (the most
# recent submission from THIS session, so concurrent agents never read each
# other's job by accident).
rec_path() {
  local ref="${1:-}"
  [[ "$ref" == "--last" || -z "$ref" ]] && ref="$(cat "$LASTD/$(session_id)" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [[ -n "$ref" ]] || return 1
  [[ -f "$BY_ID/$ref" ]] && { print -r -- "$JOBS/$(cat "$BY_ID/$ref")"; return 0 }
  [[ -f "$JOBS/$ref" ]] && { print -r -- "$JOBS/$ref"; return 0 }
  return 1
}

# ONE socket for the whole machine, independent of cwd, project or shell.
# That single fact is what makes the queue global across agents and repos.
export TS_SOCKET="${QUEUE_SOCKET:-/tmp/queue-${UID}.sock}"
export TS_MAXFINISHED="${QUEUE_MAXFINISHED:-50}"

# QUEUE_BUDGET counted UNITS against a weight table; QUEUE_SLOTS counts JOBS.
# Reading an old budget AS a slot count would be a silent 12x over-admission --
# `QUEUE_BUDGET=12` would become twelve concurrent suites, the exact disaster
# this tool exists to prevent, and it would look like nothing was wrong until
# the box started swapping. So it is ignored, loudly, never reinterpreted.
if [[ -z "${QUEUE_SLOTS:-}" && -n "${QUEUE_BUDGET:-}" ]]; then
  print -u2 "$SELF: QUEUE_BUDGET (and TESTQ_BUDGET) is obsolete and was IGNORED -- it counted weight units, and slots count whole jobs. Using the default 1. Set QUEUE_SLOTS in ~/.zshenv."
fi
# Same shape, one layer out: a per-job weight override has no meaning when every
# job costs exactly one slot. `--solo` is the only remaining cost dial.
if [[ -n "${QUEUE_WEIGHT:-}" ]]; then
  print -u2 "$SELF: QUEUE_WEIGHT (and TESTQ_WEIGHT) is obsolete and was IGNORED -- there are no weights. Use --solo to run a job alone."
fi
readonly SLOTS="${QUEUE_SLOTS:-1}"
[[ "$SLOTS" == <-> && "$SLOTS" -ge 1 ]] \
  || die "QUEUE_SLOTS must be a positive integer, got '$SLOTS'"
readonly HEARTBEAT="${QUEUE_HEARTBEAT:-30}"
readonly QUIET="${QUEUE_QUIET:-}"
# How many times longer than us a queued job ahead has to be before we may jump
# it. Validated here rather than in the supervisor, which runs in the background
# where a `die` would be invisible.
readonly QUICK_RATIO="${QUEUE_QUICK_RATIO:-3}"
[[ "$QUICK_RATIO" == <-> && "$QUICK_RATIO" -ge 1 ]] \
  || die "QUEUE_QUICK_RATIO must be a positive integer, got '$QUICK_RATIO'"

# `ts -S` starts the daemon if it isn't up, and pins the slot count if it is.
# Both are idempotent, so this is safe to call on every invocation.
#
# The "is task-spooler installed" check lives HERE rather than at the top of the
# script on purpose: --help must answer on a box with no queue daemon at all.
ensure_server() {
  command -v ts >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || die "task-spooler is not installed -- run: brew install task-spooler"
  ts -S "$SLOTS" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || die "could not start or reach the queue daemon at $TS_SOCKET"
}

# ── queue introspection ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `ts -l` is a header line plus one line per job; state is the second column.
# Everything here parses by COLUMN, never by searching the command text, so a
# job whose command happens to contain the word "running" cannot skew a count.

# How many jobs will consume capacity before a NEW submission does.
queue_depth() {
  ts -l 2>/dev/null | tail -n +2 |
    awk '$2=="queued" || $2=="running" {n++} END{print n+0}'
}

# Jobs strictly AHEAD of job $1 in run order.
#
# THE BUG THIS REPLACES: the old version counted every unfinished job except
# your own, so a job submitted BEHIND you also incremented your count. The
# number grew as the queue grew, which reads as losing ground and makes a
# bounded wait feel unbounded. Run order is job-id order, so only lower ids can
# be ahead of us.
queue_ahead_of() {
  ts -l 2>/dev/null | tail -n +2 |
    awk -v me="$1" '($2=="queued" || $2=="running") && ($1+0) < (me+0) {n++} END{print n+0}'
}

ids_in_state() {
  ts -l 2>/dev/null | tail -n +2 | awk -v s="$1" '$2==s {print $1}'
}

state_of_id() { ts -s "$1" 2>/dev/null || true }

# Empty means "not enqueued yet" -- NOT "gone". A caller must not treat the two
# as the same or it will stop watching before the job ever appears.
id_by_label() {
  ts -l 2>/dev/null | tail -n +2 | awk -v l="[$1]" 'index($0, l) {print $1; exit}'
}

# ── looking inside a `zsh -c` wrapper ────────────────────────────────────────
# NOTHING SCHEDULES ON THIS ANY MORE. Weighing a command used to be the first
# thing that happened here, and unwrapping existed to feed the classifier. Both
# the classifier and its selftest are gone: a job's cost is 1 slot (or all of
# them, with --solo) and no branch of this file asks what the command IS.
#
# What survives needs the unwrap for two cosmetic-but-load-bearing reasons,
# and it is still reached often because `queue 'cd /repo && cargo test'` (the
# supported answer to the `&&` trap) re-wraps into exactly this shape:
#   - effective_cwd(): a leading `cd` INSIDE the string means the directory the
#     job builds in is not this process's $PWD. Anti-affinity compares target
#     dirs, so reading the wrong one promotes a job that really does contend.
#   - display_cmd(): `zsh -c cd /very/long/path && cargo …` truncated to 60
#     characters in a heartbeat line is all wrapper and no command.
#
# `${(z)}` is zsh's OWN parser, which is the point -- quotes are honoured (so
# `rg -n "cargo test"` stays one word) and `&&`, `||`, `|`, `;` come back as
# their own words. `${(Q)}` then strips one level of quoting. Depth is bounded
# at 3 for the nested `zsh -c "zsh -c …"` case; unbalanced quotes do not error,
# they just produce a ragged token that matches nothing -- harmless now that the
# only consumers are a cwd guess and a display string.
typeset -ga FLAT
flatten_argv() {
  setopt localoptions extended_glob
  FLAT=("$@")
  local -a next; local depth=0 i changed
  while (( depth++ < 3 )); do
    next=(); changed=0
    for (( i = 1; i <= $#FLAT; i++ )); do
      case "${FLAT[i]:t}" in
        sh|zsh|bash|dash|ksh)
          if [[ "${FLAT[i+1]:-}" == -[a-z]#c && -n "${FLAT[i+2]:-}" ]]; then   # -c, -lc, -ic
            next+=(${(Q)${(z)FLAT[i+2]}}); (( i += 2 )); changed=1; continue
          fi ;;
      esac
      next+=("${FLAT[i]}")
    done
    FLAT=("${next[@]}")
    (( changed )) || break
  done
}

# WHERE THE JOB ACTUALLY RUNS, which is not necessarily $PWD. The hook wraps the
# whole command, so `cd /other/worktree && cargo test` carries its own `cd`
# INSIDE the string: this process's $PWD is wherever the agent happened to be.
# Anti-affinity compares target dirs, so reading $PWD would compare the wrong
# pair and promote a job that really does contend for the same target/ lock.
# Only a LEADING `cd <dir>` counts, and only if the dir exists -- guessing a
# path is worse than honestly falling back to where we are.
effective_cwd() {
  flatten_argv "$@"
  [[ "${FLAT[1]:-}" == cd && -n "${FLAT[2]:-}" && -d "${FLAT[2]}" ]] \
    && { print -r -- "${FLAT[2]:A}"; return }
  print -r -- "$PWD"
}

# The flattened command with the wrapper and any leading `cd` stripped -- what
# the job actually runs. The record keeps the literal argv under `cmd` (that is
# what was submitted, and it must stay verbatim); this is the human-facing form,
# because `zsh -c cd /very/long/path/to/worktree && cargo …` truncated to 60
# characters in a heartbeat line is all wrapper and no command.
display_cmd() {
  flatten_argv "$@"
  local -a d=("${FLAT[@]}")
  if [[ "${d[1]:-}" == cd && -n "${d[2]:-}" ]]; then
    d=("${d[@]:2}")
    [[ "${d[1]:-}" == '&&' || "${d[1]:-}" == ';' ]] && d=("${d[@]:1}")
  fi
  print -r -- "${(j: :)d}"
}

# ── duration history ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every completion appends one row, so the queue can answer "is this worth
# waiting for" rather than just "please wait".
history_append() {
  # $'\t', NOT "\t": `print -r` means "raw", which DISABLES escape processing,
  # so "$1\t$2" wrote a literal backslash-t and every row came out as a single
  # field. `history_median` reads with `awk -F'\t'`, where awk expands the
  # escape into a real tab -- so $1 was the whole line, never matched a cmdkey,
  # and every ETA silently read as unknown. It failed in the quiet direction:
  # the heartbeat still printed a blocker line, just permanently without the
  # "median Ns / est. Ns" that is the entire reason this history exists.
  # Present since the history was added; found 2026-08-03.
  print -r -- "$1"$'\t'"$2"$'\t'"$3" >>"$HISTORY" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# Median duration for a command key. There used to be a second lookup here, by
# weight class, so an unseen command could still borrow an ETA from others of
# its kind; with the classifier gone there are no kinds, and a command is only
# comparable to its own past runs. Absent history returns FAILURE -- callers
# must render that as "unknown", never as 0, or a first-ever job would
# advertise an ETA of zero seconds.
history_median() {
  local cmdkey="$1" vals
  [[ -r "$HISTORY" ]] || return 1
  vals=$(awk -F'\t' -v k="$cmdkey" '$1==k {print $2}' "$HISTORY" 2>/dev/null)
  [[ -n "$vals" ]] || return 1
  print -r -- "$vals" | sort -n | awk '{a[NR]=$1} END{print a[int((NR+1)/2)]}'
}

# WHICH REPO a job belongs to, not which directory it was launched from. The git
# COMMON dir is shared by every worktree of one repo, so it survives the way work
# is actually organised here: a worktree is created for an issue and deleted with
# it, and keying on the worktree gave each new one an empty history -- no median,
# so no ETA in the heartbeat and nothing for promote_if_quick to decide on, for
# a command that had already run twenty times next door.
#
# `rev-parse` may answer RELATIVELY (`.git` from the main worktree), and a
# relative path would key on whatever cwd the reader happened to have. `:A` fixes
# that and also collapses /var vs /private/var, so a `cd`-chain and a plain
# submission in the same directory agree. Outside a repo there is nothing better
# than the directory itself.
repo_identity() {
  local base="${1:-$PWD}" common
  common=$(git -C "$base" rev-parse --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null) && [[ -n "$common" ]] \
    || { print -r -- "${base:A}"; return }
  [[ "$common" == /* ]] || common="$base/$common"
  print -r -- "${common:A}"
}

# A stable identity for "this command, in this repo", used to look up how long
# this kind of job usually takes.
#
# BOTH halves are the resolved forms, never what was typed. The submitter's $PWD
# is the wrong directory whenever the command carries its own `cd` -- which is
# the SUPPORTED answer to the `&&` trap, so it is the common shape, not an edge
# case -- and the raw argv is wrong for the same reason: `zsh -c 'cd /wt-9 &&
# just ci-fast'` and `just ci-fast` are one command with one runtime, and keying
# them apart split every measurement this file makes into unusable fragments.
#
# Rows written under the old key simply orphan; prune_state ages them out.
cmd_key() {
  print -r -- "$(repo_identity "${EFFECTIVE_CWD:-$PWD}") $(display_cmd "$@")" \
    | shasum | cut -c1-12
}

# Where cargo will take its exclusive artifact lock. Two jobs sharing this
# cannot really compile at once whatever the queue says. Resolved from
# EFFECTIVE_CWD, not $PWD -- see effective_cwd() for why those differ.
target_dir_of() {
  [[ -n "${CARGO_TARGET_DIR:-}" ]] && { print -r -- "$CARGO_TARGET_DIR"; return }
  local base="${EFFECTIVE_CWD:-$PWD}" root
  root=$(git -C "$base" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || root="$base"
  print -r -- "$root/target"
}

# ── abandon reaping ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An agent's shell tool SIGKILLs its process group on timeout, so no trap fires
# and the queued job outlives its submitter -- it eventually runs, for nobody,
# holding capacity someone else could use. Every submission sweeps for those.
#
# A job with no record predates this version or came from elsewhere: leave it
# alone. Reaping something we cannot identify is worse than leaving it.
reap_abandoned() {
  local id recfile pid
  for id in ${(f)"$(ids_in_state queued)"}; do
    recfile=$(rec_path "$id" 2>/dev/null) || continue
    pid=$(rec_get "$recfile" pid) || continue
    kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null && continue
    ts -r "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1 && rec_put "$recfile" reaped "$(date +%s)"
  done
}

# ── deduplication of concurrent identical work ───────────────────────────────
# When N agents verify the same branch, several submit a byte-identical command
# against an unchanged tree. Only the first needs to run; the rest can watch.
#
# WHAT "IDENTICAL" HAS TO MEAN, given that returning a stale result the caller
# cannot distinguish from a fresh one is the worst thing this tool could do:
# same cwd, same argv, same HEAD, same dirty-file fingerprint, same build-shaping
# environment. All of that goes INTO the key, so a tree that has moved simply
# produces a different key and coalesces with nothing -- there is no separate
# "is it still valid" check to get wrong.
#
# THE CONTRACT, precisely: a follower gets the result of a run of this exact
# command against this exact tree state, started at the moment its own run would
# have started. Coalescing only ever happens with a leader that is still
# running -- the leader removes its claim the instant it finishes, so this is a
# concurrency optimisation, never a result cache that can serve yesterday's
# answer.
#
# NOT ELIGIBLE, deliberately: interactive (tty) callers, which keep the fully
# transparent path; anything outside a git repo, since without a fingerprint
# there is no way to know the tree is unchanged and guessing is not an option.
readonly DEDUP="$STATE/dedup"

dedup_eligible() {
  [[ -z "${QUEUE_NO_DEDUP:-}" ]] || return 1
  [[ ! -t 1 ]] || return 1
  git rev-parse --show-toplevel >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# HEAD plus the identity of every dirty/untracked file. Size+mtime rather than
# content: cheap, and it cannot miss an edit, which is the direction that
# matters. An unreadable or enormous working tree returns failure -- and failure
# means "run it for real", never "assume unchanged".
tree_fingerprint() {
  local head files n
  head=$(git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  files=$(git ls-files -m -o --exclude-standard 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  n=$(print -r -- "$files" | grep -c . || true)
  (( n > 1000 )) && return 1
  # `if`, not `[[ ... ]] &&`: a false `&&` would make the brace group exit
  # non-zero, and under pipe_fail that becomes the whole pipeline's status --
  # so a perfectly CLEAN tree would report "no fingerprint" and silently
  # disable dedup exactly when it is most likely to apply.
  {
    print -r -- "$head"
    if [[ -n "$files" ]]; then
      print -r -- "$files" | while IFS= read -r f; do
        stat -f '%N %m %z' -- "$f" 2>/dev/null || print -r -- "$f UNREADABLE"
      done
    fi
  } | shasum | cut -c1-16
}

# Variables that change what a build PRODUCES. Two runs that differ here are not
# the same job however identical their argv.
dedup_env() {
  local v
  for v in CARGO_TARGET_DIR CARGO_INCREMENTAL RUSTFLAGS RUSTDOCFLAGS \
           RUSTC_WRAPPER RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN RUST_LOG CARGO_BUILD_JOBS; do
    print -r -- "$v=$(printenv "$v" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  done
}

# A trailing `2>&1` is the ONE thing normalised out of the dedup key, and only at
# the very end of the command string. Merging the two streams changes what the
# CALLER sees; it cannot change what the run produces, and a follower is replayed
# the leader's separately-captured .out and .err either way -- so it receives the
# same bytes whether or not the leader wrote the redirect.
#
# NOTHING ELSE QUALIFIES, and the boundary is the point: `> file` puts output
# somewhere the leader never wrote it, and `2>/dev/null` says the caller does not
# want stderr handed back at all. Two jobs differing there are different work.
#
# OBSERVED: two `just ci-fast` submissions against one unchanged tree, one of
# them written with a trailing `2>&1`. Byte-identity said they were different
# jobs, so both suites ran.
strip_trailing_merge() {
  setopt localoptions extended_glob
  local s="$1"
  while [[ "$s" == *[[:space:]]##"2>&1" ]]; do
    s="${s%%[[:space:]]##"2>&1"}"
  done
  print -r -- "$s"
}

# The argv as the dedup hash should see it. `set --` rather than an array so the
# quoting expression below stays the one the record uses, character for
# character. Only the `zsh -c <string>` shape (what a quoted submission becomes)
# and a lone string are touched.
dedup_argv() {
  setopt localoptions extended_glob
  if [[ $# -eq 3 && "${1:t}" == (sh|zsh|bash|dash|ksh) && "$2" == -[a-z]#c ]]; then
    set -- "$1" "$2" "$(strip_trailing_merge "$3")"
  elif [[ $# -eq 1 ]]; then
    set -- "$(strip_trailing_merge "$1")"
  fi
  print -r -- "${(j: :)${(q+)@}}"
}

dedup_key() {
  local fp
  fp=$(tree_fingerprint) || return 1
  {
    print -r -- "$PWD"
    dedup_argv "$@"
    print -r -- "$fp"
    dedup_env
  } | shasum | cut -c1-24
}

# Attach to a running leader: stream back exactly what it produced and report
# its code in FOLLOW_RC. Returns 1 if there is nothing safe to attach to, in
# which case the caller runs the job for real.
#
# The result comes back through a global rather than stdout on purpose: this
# function's whole job is to WRITE the leader's output to the real stdout and
# stderr, so it must not be called in a command substitution that would capture
# exactly the bytes it exists to emit.
FOLLOW_RC=0
dedup_follow() {
  local key="$1" leader lrec lpid code
  leader=$(cat "$DEDUP/$key/leader" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  [[ -n "$leader" ]] || return 1
  lrec="$JOBS/$leader"
  [[ -r "$lrec" ]] || return 1
  lpid=$(rec_get "$lrec" pid) || return 1

  say "identical job already running (${leader}); attaching instead of queueing a duplicate"
  while :; do
    code=$(rec_get "$lrec" exit) && break
    # A leader that died without recording an exit leaves nothing to attach to.
    kill -0 "$lpid" 2>/dev/null || return 1
    sleep 1
  done

  [[ -r "$JOBS/$leader.out" ]] && cat "$JOBS/$leader.out"
  [[ -r "$JOBS/$leader.err" ]] && cat "$JOBS/$leader.err" >&2
  rec_put "$RECORD" deduped_from "$leader"
  rec_put "$RECORD" started  "$(date +%s)"
  rec_put "$RECORD" finished "$(date +%s)"
  rec_put "$RECORD" exit "$code"
  FOLLOW_RC="$code"
}

# ── the waiting message ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "waiting for a free slot" does not say who holds it, and the decision the
# caller is actually making is "is this worth waiting for". So name the blocker
# and estimate, from its own history, how much longer it has.
describe_blocker() {
  local id recfile cmd cwd started key med elapsed est desc now
  id=$(ids_in_state running | head -1)
  [[ -n "$id" ]] || return 1
  now=$(date +%s)

  recfile=$(rec_path "$id" 2>/dev/null) || { print -r -- "job $id"; return 0 }
  # `display`/`effective_cwd` over `cmd`/`cwd`: the raw record holds the literal
  # `zsh -c 'cd /long/path && …'` the hook submitted, so 60 characters of it are
  # all wrapper, and its cwd is the submitter's, not the one being built in.
  # Records written by an older queue have neither key -- fall back rather than
  # print nothing.
  cmd=$(rec_get "$recfile" display) || cmd=$(rec_get "$recfile" cmd) || cmd="?"
  cwd=$(rec_get "$recfile" effective_cwd) || cwd=$(rec_get "$recfile" cwd) || cwd=""
  started=$(rec_get "$recfile" started) || started=""
  key=$(rec_get "$recfile" cmdkey)  || key=""

  desc="job $id (${cwd:t}: ${cmd:0:60}"
  if [[ -n "$started" ]]; then
    elapsed=$(( now - started ))
    desc="$desc, running ${elapsed}s"
  fi
  if med=$(history_median "$key"); then
    desc="$desc; median ${med}s"
    if [[ -n "$started" ]]; then
      est=$(( med - elapsed ))
      (( est < 0 )) && est=0
    fi
  fi
  desc="$desc)"
  [[ -n "${est:-}" ]] && desc="$desc — est. ${est}s"
  print -r -- "$desc"
}

say() { [[ -n "$QUIET" ]] || print -u2 "$SELF: $*" }

# ── dropping a job ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY THIS IS NOT "just use ts -r". A bare `ts -r <id>` typed by hand talks to
# task-spooler's DEFAULT socket, not this queue's, so it is addressed to a daemon
# that has never heard of the job; it fails with "The job cannot be removed",
# which reads as a stuck job rather than a misaddressed one. This file already
# exports the right TS_SOCKET, so the flag exists to make that impossible to get
# wrong -- not as a convenience alias.
#
# It never kills. Dropping a job that has not started costs nothing and the
# submitter finds out immediately; killing one that has been compiling for eight
# minutes is a different act with a different blast radius. So the remedy is
# PRINTED, exactly, and a human decides to run it.
cancel_job() {
  local ref="${1:-}" id recfile="" disp st
  [[ -n "$ref" ]] || die "--cancel needs a job id, a label, or --last"

  # A bare number is used as-is, so a job submitted by something other than this
  # wrapper is still cancellable -- it holds a slot like any other.
  if [[ "$ref" == <-> ]]; then
    id="$ref"
    recfile=$(rec_path "$ref" 2>/dev/null) || recfile=""
  else
    recfile=$(rec_path "$ref" 2>/dev/null) \
      || die "no job matches '$ref' -- '$SELF -l' lists the ids"
    id=$(rec_get "$recfile" id) \
      || die "'$ref' has no job id recorded, so it never reached the queue"
  fi

  st=$(state_of_id "$id")
  case "$st" in
    queued) ;;
    running)
      die "job $id is already RUNNING -- --cancel never kills. To stop it anyway: TS_SOCKET=$TS_SOCKET ts -k $id" ;;
    *)
      die "job $id is not queued (state: ${st:-unknown}); nothing to cancel" ;;
  esac

  ts -r "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || die "could not remove job $id from the queue at $TS_SOCKET"

  if [[ -n "$recfile" ]]; then
    disp=$(rec_get "$recfile" display) || disp=$(rec_get "$recfile" cmd) || disp="?"
    rec_put "$recfile" cancelled "$(date +%s)"
    print "$SELF: cancelled queued job $id ($disp)"
  else
    print "$SELF: cancelled queued job $id (no record filed under that id, so its command is not named here)"
  fi
}

# ── triage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REPORT ONLY, deliberately. Two of the three findings are heuristics: sharing a
# target dir is overlap, not proof of duplication, and real subset detection
# (module tests ⊆ full suite) needs test-filter semantics nothing in this file
# has. Acting on a heuristic means silently discarding somebody's work; printing
# the ids and the exact command to run does not. A job with no record is named as
# unidentifiable rather than guessed at, the same rule reap_abandoned follows.
#
# A leading `RUSTFLAGS=…` is environment, not the command, so it is stripped
# before asking what the job actually is -- otherwise one assignment hides a
# clippy run from the "needn't be queued" check.
strip_leading_assignments() {
  setopt localoptions extended_glob
  local s="$1"
  while [[ "$s" == [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]#=*[[:space:]]* ]]; do
    s="${s#*[[:space:]]}"
  done
  print -r -- "$s"
}

# Same category the unqueued-heavy guard deliberately does NOT flag: these are
# meant to stay instant, and queueing one parks it behind a suite for nothing.
needs_no_queue() {
  local d
  d="$(strip_leading_assignments "$1")"
  [[ "$d" == just[[:space:]]lint*                             ]] && return 0
  [[ "$d" == cargo[[:space:]](clippy|check|build)             ]] && return 0
  [[ "$d" == cargo[[:space:]](clippy|check|build)[[:space:]]* ]] && return 0
  return 1
}

triage_queue() {
  local line id st lbl recfile cwd disp tdir
  local table="" unknown="" report="" n=0
  local -a fields

  # The LABEL as well as the id, because the by-id index is written by the
  # background supervisor a beat AFTER `ts` starts listing the job -- so a
  # by-id-only join reports a job this very wrapper submitted a moment ago as
  # unidentifiable, which is a wrong answer that looks like a finding. The
  # record is filed under its label BEFORE submission, so the label always
  # resolves and "unidentifiable" keeps its real meaning: not ours.
  for line in ${(f)"$(ts -l 2>/dev/null | tail -n +2 | awk '$2=="queued"||$2=="running" {
        lbl=""; if (match($0, /\[[^][]+\]/)) lbl = substr($0, RSTART+1, RLENGTH-2)
        print $1 "\t" $2 "\t" lbl }')"}; do
    [[ -n "$line" ]] || continue
    fields=("${(@ps:\t:)line}")
    id="${fields[1]}"
    st="${fields[2]}"
    lbl="${fields[3]:-}"
    (( n++ ))
    recfile=$(rec_path "$id" 2>/dev/null) || recfile=""
    if [[ -z "$recfile" && -n "$lbl" ]]; then
      recfile=$(rec_path "$lbl" 2>/dev/null) || recfile=""
    fi
    if [[ -z "$recfile" ]]; then
      unknown+="$id "
      continue
    fi
    cwd=$(rec_get "$recfile" effective_cwd) || cwd=$(rec_get "$recfile" cwd) || cwd="?"
    disp=$(rec_get "$recfile" display)      || disp=$(rec_get "$recfile" cmd) || disp="?"
    tdir=$(rec_get "$recfile" target_dir)   || tdir="?"
    # Normalised the same way the dedup key is, so the two agree about what
    # "the same command" means.
    table+="$id"$'\t'"$st"$'\t'"$cwd"$'\t'"$(strip_trailing_merge "$disp")"$'\t'"$tdir"$'\n'
  done

  report+=$(print -r -- "$table" | awk -F'\t' -v self="$SELF" '
    NF>=5 { k = $3 SUBSEP $4; ids[k] = ids[k] " " $1; n[k]++; cmd[k] = $4 }
    END { for (k in n) if (n[k] > 1) {
            s = substr(ids[k], 2); c = split(s, a, " ")
            line = sprintf("duplicates: jobs %s all run `%s` in the same directory -- cancel the later one(s):", s, cmd[k])
            for (i = 2; i <= c; i++) line = line sprintf(" %s --cancel %s", self, a[i])
            print line } }')$'\n'

  report+=$(print -r -- "$table" | awk -F'\t' '
    NF>=5 { t = $5; g[t] = g[t] " " $1; n[t]++
            if ($2 == "queued") q[t] = 1
            k = t SUBSEP $3 SUBSEP $4
            if (!(k in seen)) { seen[k] = 1; distinct[t]++ } }
    END { for (t in n) if (n[t] > 1 && (t in q) && distinct[t] > 1)
            printf "same target dir: jobs %s all build in %s -- possible subset/overlap, review\n",
                   substr(g[t], 2), t }')$'\n'

  for line in ${(f)table}; do
    [[ -n "$line" ]] || continue
    fields=("${(@ps:\t:)line}")
    needs_no_queue "${fields[4]}" \
      && report+="needn't be queued: job ${fields[1]} (${fields[4]}) -- run it unqueued; not queueing it is what keeps it instant"$'\n'
  done

  [[ -n "$unknown" ]] \
    && report+="unidentifiable: job(s) ${unknown% } have no queue record -- submitted outside $SELF, so nothing is claimed about them"$'\n'

  # Honest empty state: silence would be indistinguishable from a triage that
  # failed to read the queue at all.
  if [[ -z "${report//[[:space:]]/}" ]]; then
    print "$SELF: nothing to flag — $n unfinished job(s), no duplicates, no shared target dirs, nothing that belongs outside the queue"
    return 0
  fi
  print -r -- "$report" | while IFS= read -r line; do
    [[ -n "$line" ]] && print -r -- "$SELF: $line"
  done
  return 0
}

# ── management subcommands ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
case "${1:-}" in
  -h|--help)
    # -E, not a BRE: BSD sed has no `\?`, so `s/^# \?//` silently matched
    # nothing and --help printed every line with its comment marker still on.
    sed -n '/^# USAGE/,/^# RE-ENTRANCY/p' "$0" | sed -E 's/^# ?//'
    exit 0 ;;
  -l|--list|status)
    ensure_server; exec ts -l ;;
  --watch)
    ensure_server
    while :; do clear; print "queue: $TS_SOCKET  ($SLOTS slot(s))"; print; ts -l; sleep 2; done ;;
  --slots)
    ensure_server
    if [[ -n "${2:-}" ]]; then
      [[ "$2" == <-> && "$2" -ge 1 ]] || die "--slots needs a positive integer, got '$2'"
      ts -S "$2" || die "could not set the slot count to $2"
      print "$SELF: slots -> $2 (this session only; export QUEUE_SLOTS=$2 to persist)"
    else
      print "$SELF: $SLOTS slot(s)  (socket: $TS_SOCKET)"
    fi
    exit 0 ;;
  --budget)
    die "--budget is obsolete: there are no weight units any more. Use --slots (whole jobs), or --solo to run one job alone." ;;
  --cancel)
    state_init; ensure_server
    cancel_job "${2:-}"
    exit 0 ;;
  --triage)
    state_init; ensure_server
    triage_queue
    exit 0 ;;
  --clear)
    ensure_server; exec ts -C ;;
  --kill)
    ts -K 2>/dev/null && print "$SELF: queue daemon stopped" || print "$SELF: no queue daemon running"
    exit 0 ;;
  --ahead)
    ensure_server; queue_depth; exit 0 ;;
  --ahead-of)
    [[ -n "${2:-}" ]] || die "--ahead-of needs a job id"
    ensure_server; queue_ahead_of "$2"; exit 0 ;;
  --status)
    state_init
    recfile=$(rec_path "${2:---last}") || die "no job record for '${2:---last}'"
    exec cat "$recfile" ;;
  --exit-code)
    state_init
    recfile=$(rec_path "${2:---last}") || die "no job record for '${2:---last}'"
    code=$(rec_get "$recfile" exit) \
      || die "that job has not finished; no exit code recorded yet"
    print -r -- "$code"
    exit 0 ;;
esac

# ── job submission ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
EVENTS=0
PRIORITY=0
SOLO=0
while true; do
  case "${1:-}" in
    --events)   EVENTS=1; shift ;;
    --priority) PRIORITY=1; shift ;;
    --solo)     SOLO=1; shift ;;
    *) break ;;
  esac
done
[[ "${1:-}" == "--" ]] && shift
[[ $# -gt 0 ]] || die "nothing to run. See '$SELF --help'."

# ONE argument carrying shell syntax is a shell command, not a program name --
# `queue 'cd /repo && cargo nextest run'`. This is the supported answer to the
# `&&` trap in the header: quoting is what keeps the operator inside our argv
# instead of splitting the command in half before we are even exec'd.
#
# A single BARE word is left alone, so `queue mytestrunner` still execs the
# binary directly rather than paying for a shell. The distinction is visible to
# the caller (did you type a space or a metacharacter?), not inferred from
# anything about what the command means.
if [[ $# -eq 1 && "$1" == *[[:space:]\;\|\&\<\>\(\)\$\`]* ]]; then
  set -- zsh -c "$1"
fi

state_init
prune_state
ensure_server
reap_abandoned

LABEL="tq-$$-${RANDOM}"
EFFECTIVE_CWD="$(effective_cwd "$@")"
# The entire scheduling decision. Nothing inspects the command.
if (( SOLO )); then COST=$SLOTS; else COST=1; fi

# An interactive submission is a human waiting at a terminal, and it is the
# most penalised caller in the system today -- background agent suites starve
# the one job someone is actually watching. Give it the priority lane.
[[ -t 1 ]] && PRIORITY=1

RECORD="$JOBS/$LABEL"
CMDKEY="$(cmd_key "$@")"
: >"$RECORD"
rec_put "$RECORD" label      "$LABEL"
rec_put "$RECORD" pid        "$$"
rec_put "$RECORD" session    "$(session_id)"
rec_put "$RECORD" cwd        "$PWD"
rec_put "$RECORD" effective_cwd "$EFFECTIVE_CWD"
rec_put "$RECORD" target_dir "$(target_dir_of)"
rec_put "$RECORD" cmd        "${(j: :)${(q+)@}}"
rec_put "$RECORD" display    "$(display_cmd "$@")"
rec_put "$RECORD" cmdkey     "$CMDKEY"
rec_put "$RECORD" cost       "$COST"
rec_put "$RECORD" slots      "$SLOTS"
rec_put "$RECORD" solo       "$SOLO"
rec_put "$RECORD" priority   "$PRIORITY"
rec_put "$RECORD" submitted  "$(date +%s)"
print -r -- "$LABEL" >"$LASTD/$(session_id)" 2>/dev/null || true

# --- events mode: detached submission, one line per state transition ---------
if (( EVENTS )); then
  id="$(QUEUE_ACTIVE=1 ts -L "$LABEL" -N "$COST" "$@")" \
    || die "could not enqueue job"
  rec_put "$RECORD" id "$id"
  print -r -- "$LABEL" >"$BY_ID/$id" 2>/dev/null || true
  (( PRIORITY )) && ts -u "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1
  print "QUEUED id=$id ahead=$(queue_ahead_of "$id") label=$LABEL slots=$SLOTS cost=$COST"
  t0=$(date +%s)
  while [[ "$(state_of_id "$id")" == "queued" ]]; do sleep 1; done
  rec_put "$RECORD" started "$(date +%s)"
  print "RUNNING id=$id waited=$(( $(date +%s) - t0 ))s"
  t1=$(date +%s)
  ts -w "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1
  rc=$?
  el=$(( $(date +%s) - t1 ))
  rec_put "$RECORD" finished "$(date +%s)"
  rec_put "$RECORD" exit "$rc"
  rec_put "$RECORD" out "$(ts -o "$id" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  history_append "$CMDKEY" "$el" "$rc"
  print "DONE id=$id exit=$rc elapsed=${el}s out=$(ts -o "$id" 2>/dev/null)"
  exit $rc
fi

# --- coalesce with an identical in-flight job, if there is one ---------------
# The claim is a directory, because mkdir is atomic: exactly one of N racing
# submitters can create it, and that one is the leader. No lock file, no window.
CAPTURE=0
DEDUP_KEY=""
if dedup_eligible && DEDUP_KEY=$(dedup_key "$@"); then
  mkdir -p "$DEDUP" 2>/dev/null || true
  if mkdir "$DEDUP/$DEDUP_KEY" 2>/dev/null; then
    print -r -- "$LABEL" >"$DEDUP/$DEDUP_KEY/leader" 2>/dev/null || true
    CAPTURE=1
  elif dedup_follow "$DEDUP_KEY"; then
    exit "$FOLLOW_RC"
  else
    # A claim with nothing usable behind it -- the leader was SIGKILLed before
    # recording anything. Take it over rather than wait on a ghost forever.
    print -r -- "$LABEL" >"$DEDUP/$DEDUP_KEY/leader" 2>/dev/null || true
    CAPTURE=1
  fi
fi

# --- normal mode: transparent, with a supervisor while we wait ---------------
#
# `ts -nf` blocks and never reveals the job id, so a background supervisor
# discovers it by label and owns everything needing that id: the record, the
# priority bump, anti-affinity promotion and the heartbeat. The parent stays a
# plain blocking call, which is what keeps the wrapper transparent.
AHEAD_AT_SUBMIT=$(queue_depth)
if (( AHEAD_AT_SUBMIT > 0 )); then
  say "$AHEAD_AT_SUBMIT job(s) ahead; blocked by $(describe_blocker || print -r -- 'a running job') ($SLOTS slot(s), this job costs $COST)"
fi

supervise() {
  local id="" waited=0 t0=$SECONDS promoted=0 tries=0 st last_ahead=999999

  # Discover our id. Polling fast matters: a priority bump only helps if it
  # lands before the scheduler picks the next job.
  while (( tries < 400 )); do
    id=$(id_by_label "$LABEL")
    [[ -n "$id" ]] && break
    sleep 0.05
    (( tries++ ))
  done
  [[ -n "$id" ]] || return 0

  rec_put "$RECORD" id "$id"
  print -r -- "$LABEL" >"$BY_ID/$id" 2>/dev/null || true
  (( PRIORITY )) && ts -u "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1

  while :; do
    st=$(state_of_id "$id")
    [[ "$st" == "running" || "$st" == "finished" || -z "$st" ]] && break

    if (( ! promoted )) && { promote_if_unblocked "$id" || promote_if_starved "$id" || promote_if_quick "$id" }; then
      promoted=1
    fi

    if (( HEARTBEAT > 0 )) && (( SECONDS - t0 >= HEARTBEAT )); then
      t0=$SECONDS
      local ahead
      ahead=$(queue_ahead_of "$id")
      # A position that grows reads as losing ground; it is also never true,
      # since only lower ids count. Clamp anyway so a daemon restart or an
      # id-reuse edge case can't make the number climb.
      (( ahead > last_ahead )) && ahead=$last_ahead
      last_ahead=$ahead
      say "still queued — ${waited}s elapsed, $ahead ahead; blocked by $(describe_blocker || print -r -- 'a running job')"
    fi
    sleep 1
    (( waited++ ))
  done
  rec_put "$RECORD" started "$(date +%s)"
}

# ANTI-AFFINITY. Cargo takes an exclusive lock on target/, so two jobs sharing
# a target dir cannot really compile at once no matter what the queue allows --
# the second one just sits on "Blocking waiting for file lock", burning
# capacity for nothing.
#
# (This is the inverse of the "batch by worktree" idea it replaces. That one
# assumed alternating worktrees loses a warm target dir, which is false here:
# there is no global CARGO_TARGET_DIR, so every worktree keeps its own.)
#
# So: if every job ahead of us wants a target dir that is already locked by a
# running job, and ours is free, we can start now and nobody starts later.
# Promotion happens at most once per job, so it cannot starve the queue.
promote_if_unblocked() {
  local id="$1" mine running_dirs="" other op qp qdir
  mine=$(rec_get "$RECORD" target_dir) || return 1

  for other in ${(f)"$(ids_in_state running)"}; do
    op=$(rec_path "$other" 2>/dev/null) || return 1   # unknown job: don't guess
    running_dirs+="$(rec_get "$op" target_dir || true)"$'\n'
  done
  [[ -n "$running_dirs" ]] || return 1
  contains_line "$running_dirs" "$mine" && return 1   # we'd contend too

  for other in ${(f)"$(ids_in_state queued)"}; do
    (( other >= id )) && continue
    qp=$(rec_path "$other" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    qdir=$(rec_get "$qp" target_dir) || return 1
    contains_line "$running_dirs" "$qdir" || return 1 # that one isn't blocked
  done

  ts -u "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# FAIR SHARE ACROSS SESSIONS. Pure FIFO lets one session's fan-out flood the
# queue: ten agents from one session submit ten suites, and another session's
# single job waits behind all ten. If our session has nothing running, and every
# job ahead of us belongs to a session that is ALREADY being served, we can go
# first without delaying anyone who isn't already getting their turn.
#
# Like the anti-affinity bump this fires at most once per job, so it reorders
# the queue a little and cannot livelock it.
promote_if_starved() {
  local id="$1" mine other op osess running_sessions=""
  mine=$(rec_get "$RECORD" session) || return 1

  for other in ${(f)"$(ids_in_state running)"}; do
    op=$(rec_path "$other" 2>/dev/null) || return 1   # unknown job: don't guess
    running_sessions+="$(rec_get "$op" session || true)"$'\n'
  done
  [[ -n "$running_sessions" ]] || return 1
  contains_line "$running_sessions" "$mine" && return 1

  for other in ${(f)"$(ids_in_state queued)"}; do
    (( other >= id )) && continue
    op=$(rec_path "$other" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    osess=$(rec_get "$op" session) || return 1
    contains_line "$running_sessions" "$osess" || return 1
  done

  ts -u "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# SHORTEST JOB FIRST, decided entirely on MEASURED durations. A two-second job
# parked behind a nine-minute suite pays 270x its own runtime to wait; letting it
# past costs the suite two seconds. That trade is only worth making when both
# numbers are known, so this rule refuses to fire on anything it has not timed.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE WEIGHT CLASSIFIER COMING BACK. That one read the command TEXT
# and decided what it must be -- a table that had to stay in sync with a second
# copy inside the hook, and that was simply wrong about anything nobody had
# taught it. Nothing here looks at what the command says. It reads
# history_median for the exact keys involved, and an unknown median BLOCKS the
# promotion rather than defaulting: a command this queue has never run is far
# likelier to be a suite than a one-liner, and guessing the other way would let
# a first-ever `cargo build --release` jump every suite on the machine.
#
# Like the other two rules it fires at most once per job -- the caller's single
# `promoted` flag is shared by all three -- which is what bounds starvation. A
# job can be jumped, but not repeatedly by an endless stream of quick ones.
promote_if_quick() {
  local id="$1" mine mykey mydir other op okey omed running_dirs="" ahead=0
  mykey=$(rec_get "$RECORD" cmdkey) || return 1
  mine=$(history_median "$mykey")   || return 1
  # A 0s median would make every ratio comparison below vacuously true.
  (( mine < 1 )) && mine=1

  mydir=$(rec_get "$RECORD" target_dir) || return 1
  # An empty state list expands to one EMPTY word, and rec_path "" means
  # `--last` -- i.e. our own record. Skip it explicitly rather than reason about
  # what conclusion that accident happens to reach.
  for other in ${(f)"$(ids_in_state running)"}; do
    [[ -n "$other" ]] || continue
    op=$(rec_path "$other" 2>/dev/null) || return 1   # unknown job: don't guess
    running_dirs+="$(rec_get "$op" target_dir || true)"$'\n'
  done
  # Jumping the queue only to sit on someone else's cargo lock helps nobody.
  contains_line "$running_dirs" "$mydir" && return 1

  for other in ${(f)"$(ids_in_state queued)"}; do
    [[ -n "$other" ]] || continue
    (( other >= id )) && continue
    ahead=1
    op=$(rec_path "$other" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    okey=$(rec_get "$op" cmdkey)        || return 1
    omed=$(history_median "$okey")      || return 1
    (( omed >= QUICK_RATIO * mine ))    || return 1
  done
  # Only QUEUED jobs can be jumped; a running one cannot be preempted, so with
  # nothing queued ahead of us there is no reordering to do.
  (( ahead )) || return 1

  ts -u "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

supervise &
SUP_PID=$!

# A queued job that outlives its submitter runs for nobody. The trap covers a
# clean kill; reap_abandoned covers SIGKILL, where no trap can fire.
cleanup() {
  (( SUP_PID > 0 )) && kill "$SUP_PID" 2>/dev/null
  [[ -n "$DEDUP_KEY" ]] && rm -rf "$DEDUP/$DEDUP_KEY" 2>/dev/null
  local id
  id=$(rec_get "$RECORD" id 2>/dev/null) || return 0
  [[ "$(state_of_id "$id")" == "queued" ]] && ts -r "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1
  return 0
}
trap cleanup INT TERM

T_START=$(date +%s)
# -n : inherit this process's stdout/stderr instead of capturing to a file
# -f : don't detach -- wait for capacity, then run right here
# Together they make the wrapper transparent: live output on the right streams,
# the real exit code, the caller's cwd, stdin and environment.
if (( CAPTURE )); then
  # A leader has to keep a copy for its followers. Both tees are members of a
  # real pipeline, so the shell waits for them -- the files are complete by the
  # time this returns, which a background `tee &` could not guarantee. The exit
  # code travels out of band through a file, since the pipeline's own status
  # belongs to tee. Streams stay separate; only their tty-ness is lost, and
  # capture is off for tty callers precisely so that never matters.
  rm -f "$JOBS/$LABEL.out" "$JOBS/$LABEL.err" "$JOBS/$LABEL.rc" 2>/dev/null
  {
    { QUEUE_ACTIVE=1 ts -nf -L "$LABEL" -N "$COST" "$@"
      print -r -- $? >"$JOBS/$LABEL.rc"
    } 2>&1 1>&3 | tee "$JOBS/$LABEL.err" >&2
  } 3>&1 | tee "$JOBS/$LABEL.out"
  rc=$(cat "$JOBS/$LABEL.rc" 2>/dev/null) || rc=1
  rec_put "$RECORD" out "$JOBS/$LABEL.out"
  rec_put "$RECORD" err "$JOBS/$LABEL.err"
else
  QUEUE_ACTIVE=1 ts -nf -L "$LABEL" -N "$COST" "$@"
  rc=$?
fi
(( SUP_PID > 0 )) && kill "$SUP_PID" 2>/dev/null
trap - INT TERM

# Release the claim before recording the exit code, so that "a follower only
# ever attaches to a leader that is still running" stays literally true.
[[ -n "$DEDUP_KEY" ]] && rm -rf "$DEDUP/$DEDUP_KEY" 2>/dev/null

T_END=$(date +%s)

# Backfill what the supervisor may not have had time to write. A job that
# starts immediately can finish before the supervisor has even found its id, so
# without this a short job would have no `id=` and no by-id index -- and then
# `queue --status <id>` would report "no job record" for a job that plainly ran.
if ! ID=$(rec_get "$RECORD" id); then
  ID=$(id_by_label "$LABEL")
  [[ -n "$ID" ]] && {
    rec_put "$RECORD" id "$ID"
    print -r -- "$LABEL" >"$BY_ID/$ID" 2>/dev/null || true
  }
fi

rec_put "$RECORD" finished "$T_END"
rec_put "$RECORD" exit "$rc"
STARTED=$(rec_get "$RECORD" started) || { STARTED=$T_START; rec_put "$RECORD" started "$T_START" }
history_append "$CMDKEY" "$(( T_END - STARTED ))" "$rc"

exit $rc
